\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces  Comparison of standard ABC methods and permutation-based variants in terms of achieved tolerance \( \varepsilon \) versus wall-clock time. The \( x \)-axis shows the time required to obtain 1,000 unique accepted particles (log-scale), and the \( y \)-axis represents the effective tolerance \( \varepsilon \) (log-scale). Lower values of \( \varepsilon \) correspond to more accurate approximations of the pseudo-posterior. Each curve represents a different algorithm. Permutation-based methods — especially Over-Sampling and Under-Matching — achieve substantial efficiency gains in runtime.}}{18}{figure.{0}.{6}}\protected@file@percent }
